#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: ggdef
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: [equality, traits, fstring]
# doc: |
#   D46's reject must survive INSIDE an interpolation. `f"{a == b}"` is where a
#   comparison is most often written, and the checker deliberately truncates
#   most interpolation errors to a whitelist of kinds worth showing — so a
#   rejection whose kind is not on that whitelist is SILENTLY SWALLOWED and the
#   program compiles and prints `false`, position-dependently, while the same
#   expression at `bool r = a == b` is refused. This seed is the guard on the
#   whitelist: drop the equality kind from it and this goes red.
# expect:
#   exit: 1
#   stdout: ""
#   reject: E_UnsupportedOperator
#!end

struct Point:
    int x
    int y

void main():
    Point a = Point(1, 2)
    Point b = Point(1, 2)
    print(f"{a == b}")
